1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:01,982
Uncountable nouns.

2
00:00:02,022 --> 00:00:03,842
Why can’t we
add s to them?

3
00:00:03,882 --> 00:00:04,920
And why can’t
they take

4
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,384
a plural verb?

5
00:00:07,337 --> 00:00:09,267
Countable
nouns are easy:

6
00:00:09,307 --> 00:00:10,948
a chair,
two chairs,

7
00:00:10,988 --> 00:00:12,225
three ideas.

8
00:00:12,265 --> 00:00:13,563
We count them
one by one,

9
00:00:13,603 --> 00:00:15,295
so they
can take s.

10
00:00:15,937 --> 00:00:17,192
But what
about water,

11
00:00:17,232 --> 00:00:19,529
rice, honesty,
or music?

12
00:00:19,569 --> 00:00:21,181
Nobody says
two waters,

13
00:00:21,221 --> 00:00:22,791
or three
honesties.

14
00:00:23,537 --> 00:00:24,849
Most books
just give you

15
00:00:24,889 --> 00:00:26,323
a list
to memorise.

16
00:00:26,363 --> 00:00:28,717
We won’t.
We’ll use logic.

17
00:00:28,757 --> 00:00:30,632
Uncountable
nouns fall into

18
00:00:30,672 --> 00:00:31,972
five categories,

19
00:00:32,012 --> 00:00:34,735
and each category
has its own reason.

20
00:00:35,370 --> 00:00:37,836
One: unit,
or mass.

21
00:00:37,876 --> 00:00:38,968
Two: material.

22
00:00:39,008 --> 00:00:40,225
Three: measured.

23
00:00:40,265 --> 00:00:41,110
Four: abstract.

24
00:00:41,150 --> 00:00:41,603
And five:

25
00:00:41,643 --> 00:00:43,112
activity,
or process.

26
00:00:43,837 --> 00:00:46,515
Let’s prove them
one at a time.

27
00:00:47,637 --> 00:00:48,484
Error one.

28
00:00:48,524 --> 00:00:50,230
‘Furniture
are expensive.’

29
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:51,277
That’s wrong.

30
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:52,738
The correct
sentence is:

31
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:54,537
‘Furniture
is expensive.’

32
00:00:54,577 --> 00:00:55,571
But why?

33
00:00:56,303 --> 00:00:57,438
Category one:

34
00:00:57,478 --> 00:00:59,298
unit, or mass.

35
00:00:59,338 --> 00:01:01,216
This kind of noun
is a unit,

36
00:01:01,256 --> 00:01:03,565
and a unit contains
many things.

37
00:01:04,203 --> 00:01:05,889
Look. A chair.

38
00:01:05,929 --> 00:01:07,459
A table. A bed.

39
00:01:07,499 --> 00:01:08,783
A sofa. A desk.

40
00:01:08,823 --> 00:01:10,265
Many individual
things.

41
00:01:10,903 --> 00:01:12,862
English groups
them together.

42
00:01:12,902 --> 00:01:14,454
It draws one
circle around

43
00:01:14,494 --> 00:01:16,101
them,
and treats the whole

44
00:01:16,141 --> 00:01:17,283
group as one unit,

45
00:01:17,323 --> 00:01:18,765
called furniture.

46
00:01:19,703 --> 00:01:21,367
And a unit
means one.

47
00:01:21,407 --> 00:01:22,725
The whole
group now works

48
00:01:22,765 --> 00:01:23,703
as one thing:

49
00:01:23,743 --> 00:01:24,406
one.

50
00:01:24,446 --> 00:01:26,317
And we can’t
add s to one.

51
00:01:26,970 --> 00:01:28,610
One is always
singular.

52
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:30,379
So the word
stays singular,

53
00:01:30,419 --> 00:01:32,120
and so does
the verb.

54
00:01:32,770 --> 00:01:34,973
But why is furniture
one unit?

55
00:01:35,013 --> 00:01:35,995
Because furniture

56
00:01:36,035 --> 00:01:38,281
is not the name
of one object.

57
00:01:38,321 --> 00:01:40,539
It’s the name
of the whole collection.

58
00:01:40,579 --> 00:01:42,371
The word comes
from ‘furnish’:

59
00:01:42,411 --> 00:01:44,616
everything you
furnish a room with.

60
00:01:45,337 --> 00:01:47,296
So the correct
sentence is:

61
00:01:47,336 --> 00:01:49,503
‘The furniture
is expensive.’

62
00:01:50,637 --> 00:01:52,316
The old school
rule says:

63
00:01:52,356 --> 00:01:54,324
furniture,
luggage,

64
00:01:54,364 --> 00:01:55,199
baggage
and crockery

65
00:01:55,239 --> 00:01:56,385
are always
singular.

66
00:01:56,425 --> 00:01:57,468
Memorise it.

67
00:01:57,508 --> 00:01:59,927
Skip that.
Here’s the logic:

68
00:01:59,967 --> 00:02:01,269
many individual
things

69
00:02:01,309 --> 00:02:03,102
are named
as one unit.

70
00:02:03,142 --> 00:02:04,328
Unit means one,

71
00:02:04,368 --> 00:02:06,251
and one is always
singular.

72
00:02:07,003 --> 00:02:09,225
The same logic
works everywhere.

73
00:02:09,265 --> 00:02:10,021
Luggage:

74
00:02:10,061 --> 00:02:11,813
bags and suitcases
together,

75
00:02:11,853 --> 00:02:13,566
one whole.
Clothing:

76
00:02:13,606 --> 00:02:14,858
shirts
and trousers,

77
00:02:14,898 --> 00:02:16,024
one category.

78
00:02:16,064 --> 00:02:16,640
Crockery:

79
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:18,945
plates and cups,
one collection.

80
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:20,801
And if you
need a number,

81
00:02:20,841 --> 00:02:22,175
count a piece:

82
00:02:22,215 --> 00:02:23,505
a piece
of furniture,

83
00:02:23,545 --> 00:02:24,735
a piece
of luggage,

84
00:02:24,775 --> 00:02:26,496
an item
of clothing.

85
00:02:27,237 --> 00:02:28,506
Now think
about it.

86
00:02:28,546 --> 00:02:30,252
Why can you say
‘two chairs’,

87
00:02:30,292 --> 00:02:31,787
but not ‘two
rice’?

88
00:02:33,137 --> 00:02:33,958
Error two.

89
00:02:33,998 --> 00:02:35,755
‘This ring
is made of golds.’

90
00:02:35,795 --> 00:02:36,532
Wrong.

91
00:02:36,572 --> 00:02:38,599
‘This ring
is made of gold.’

92
00:02:39,337 --> 00:02:40,499
Category two:

93
00:02:40,539 --> 00:02:41,594
material.

94
00:02:41,634 --> 00:02:43,128
A material
is what something

95
00:02:43,168 --> 00:02:44,272
is made from.

96
00:02:44,312 --> 00:02:45,759
It’s a substance.

97
00:02:46,403 --> 00:02:48,542
Take gold.
It’s a substance,

98
00:02:48,582 --> 00:02:49,826
and it’s used
to make many

99
00:02:49,866 --> 00:02:50,852
different things:

100
00:02:50,892 --> 00:02:53,401
a ring, a coin,
a crown.

101
00:02:54,037 --> 00:02:55,561
We can count
the things:

102
00:02:55,601 --> 00:02:57,568
one ring,
two coins.

103
00:02:57,608 --> 00:02:58,939
But the substance
itself

104
00:02:58,979 --> 00:03:01,028
is measured,
not counted.

105
00:03:01,068 --> 00:03:03,051
We weigh gold
in grams.

106
00:03:03,803 --> 00:03:05,315
So a material
noun,

107
00:03:05,355 --> 00:03:06,705
used
as a material,

108
00:03:06,745 --> 00:03:08,252
isn’t made plural.

109
00:03:08,292 --> 00:03:10,465
‘This ring
is made of gold.’

110
00:03:11,603 --> 00:03:13,400
The old school
rule says:

111
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:15,297
brick, sand, milk,

112
00:03:15,337 --> 00:03:17,298
glass and water
are always singular.

113
00:03:17,338 --> 00:03:18,414
Memorise it.

114
00:03:18,454 --> 00:03:20,547
Skip that.
The logic:

115
00:03:20,587 --> 00:03:22,234
it’s the material
used to make

116
00:03:22,274 --> 00:03:23,127
other things,

117
00:03:23,167 --> 00:03:24,388
so you measure
it rather than

118
00:03:24,428 --> 00:03:25,561
count it.

119
00:03:26,303 --> 00:03:28,619
Silver:
used for jewellery,

120
00:03:28,659 --> 00:03:30,177
measured,
not counted.

121
00:03:30,217 --> 00:03:31,294
Wood:
used for furniture,

122
00:03:31,334 --> 00:03:33,256
measured
in cubic metres.

123
00:03:33,296 --> 00:03:35,012
Water:
used for drinking,

124
00:03:35,052 --> 00:03:36,685
measured
in litres.

125
00:03:37,337 --> 00:03:38,929
One careful note.

126
00:03:38,969 --> 00:03:39,988
When you
mean separate

127
00:03:40,028 --> 00:03:41,662
objects,
you can count them:

128
00:03:41,702 --> 00:03:43,101
a wall made
of bricks,

129
00:03:43,141 --> 00:03:45,173
or two glasses
of water.

130
00:03:45,213 --> 00:03:46,337
But as a material,

131
00:03:46,377 --> 00:03:47,646
it’s brick
and glass,

132
00:03:47,686 --> 00:03:48,919
with no s.

133
00:03:49,670 --> 00:03:51,035
Now think
about it.

134
00:03:51,075 --> 00:03:52,336
Do we say
‘one milk’,

135
00:03:52,376 --> 00:03:54,124
or ‘one litre
of milk’?

136
00:03:55,470 --> 00:03:56,269
Error three.

137
00:03:56,309 --> 00:03:58,292
‘I ate too many
rice for lunch.’

138
00:03:58,332 --> 00:03:58,976
Wrong.

139
00:03:59,016 --> 00:04:01,172
‘I ate too much
rice for lunch.’

140
00:04:01,903 --> 00:04:03,233
Category three:

141
00:04:03,273 --> 00:04:04,204
measured.

142
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:06,061
Food and substances
like rice

143
00:04:06,101 --> 00:04:07,718
are measured
by amount,

144
00:04:07,758 --> 00:04:10,173
not counted
as separate items.

145
00:04:10,803 --> 00:04:13,424
Rice comes as many
tiny grains.

146
00:04:13,464 --> 00:04:15,439
We don’t count
them one by one.

147
00:04:15,479 --> 00:04:17,461
We measure
the amount instead:

148
00:04:17,501 --> 00:04:20,068
a cup of rice,
a kilo of rice,

149
00:04:20,108 --> 00:04:21,569
a plate of rice.

150
00:04:22,303 --> 00:04:24,587
Measured,
not counted,

151
00:04:24,627 --> 00:04:26,420
so it always takes
a singular verb.

152
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:28,533
‘This rice
is delicious.’

153
00:04:29,670 --> 00:04:31,096
Material and food
nouns can

154
00:04:31,136 --> 00:04:31,865
look alike,

155
00:04:31,905 --> 00:04:33,371
so here’s
the test:

156
00:04:33,411 --> 00:04:34,745
do we buy it
in a measured

157
00:04:34,785 --> 00:04:35,870
amount,
like a kilo

158
00:04:35,910 --> 00:04:38,130
or a litre,
and not in pieces?

159
00:04:38,170 --> 00:04:40,079
If yes,
it’s uncountable.

160
00:04:40,119 --> 00:04:41,983
The old school
rule says food,

161
00:04:42,023 --> 00:04:43,501
meat, sugar, salt,

162
00:04:43,541 --> 00:04:45,867
tea and bread
are always singular.

163
00:04:45,907 --> 00:04:47,536
The logic:
they’re measured,

164
00:04:47,576 --> 00:04:48,788
not counted.

165
00:04:49,537 --> 00:04:50,113
Bread:

166
00:04:50,153 --> 00:04:52,108
measured in loaves
or slices.

167
00:04:52,148 --> 00:04:54,179
Milk:
measured in litres.

168
00:04:54,219 --> 00:04:56,367
Salt:
measured by weight.

169
00:04:57,103 --> 00:04:58,506
Now think
about it.

170
00:04:58,546 --> 00:05:00,043
Can you say
‘two rice’,

171
00:05:00,083 --> 00:05:01,283
or do you need
to say ‘two

172
00:05:01,323 --> 00:05:02,829
kilos of rice’?

173
00:05:04,170 --> 00:05:05,030
Error four.

174
00:05:05,070 --> 00:05:07,217
‘He has many
knowledges about art.’

175
00:05:07,257 --> 00:05:08,084
Wrong.

176
00:05:08,124 --> 00:05:09,119
‘He has a lot
of knowledge

177
00:05:09,159 --> 00:05:10,328
about art.’

178
00:05:11,070 --> 00:05:12,227
Category four:

179
00:05:12,267 --> 00:05:13,370
abstract.

180
00:05:13,410 --> 00:05:14,804
An abstract
noun has no

181
00:05:14,844 --> 00:05:16,102
physical form.

182
00:05:16,142 --> 00:05:18,092
It can’t be
held or touched.

183
00:05:18,737 --> 00:05:20,484
It’s an idea,
a quality,

184
00:05:20,524 --> 00:05:21,631
or a state.

185
00:05:21,671 --> 00:05:22,954
Knowledge,
honesty,

186
00:05:22,994 --> 00:05:24,496
advice, happiness.

187
00:05:24,536 --> 00:05:25,694
You can’t put
them on a table

188
00:05:25,734 --> 00:05:27,055
and count them.

189
00:05:27,803 --> 00:05:29,461
You experience it,

190
00:05:29,501 --> 00:05:30,476
show it,
or possess it,

191
00:05:30,516 --> 00:05:32,016
but you never
count it.

192
00:05:32,056 --> 00:05:33,249
So it’s singular:

193
00:05:33,289 --> 00:05:35,281
‘Her knowledge
is impressive.’

194
00:05:36,403 --> 00:05:37,851
The old school
rule says

195
00:05:37,891 --> 00:05:39,448
advice,
information,

196
00:05:39,488 --> 00:05:40,683
evidence
and knowledge

197
00:05:40,723 --> 00:05:42,220
are always
singular.

198
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:44,291
Skip that.
The logic:

199
00:05:44,331 --> 00:05:45,957
we can’t physically
hold them,

200
00:05:45,997 --> 00:05:47,511
or count them
directly.

201
00:05:47,551 --> 00:05:48,564
Need a number?

202
00:05:48,604 --> 00:05:49,743
A piece of advice,

203
00:05:49,783 --> 00:05:51,169
a piece
of information,

204
00:05:51,209 --> 00:05:52,737
a piece
of evidence.

205
00:05:53,470 --> 00:05:54,852
Now think
about it.

206
00:05:54,892 --> 00:05:56,478
Can you hold
honesty in your

207
00:05:56,518 --> 00:05:58,307
hand, or can you
only show it,

208
00:05:58,347 --> 00:06:00,612
have it,
or experience it?

209
00:06:01,937 --> 00:06:02,941
Error five.

210
00:06:02,981 --> 00:06:05,084
‘She does many
works every day.’

211
00:06:05,124 --> 00:06:05,869
Wrong.

212
00:06:05,909 --> 00:06:08,071
‘She does a lot
of work every day.’

213
00:06:08,803 --> 00:06:10,175
Category five:

214
00:06:10,215 --> 00:06:12,413
activity,
or process.

215
00:06:12,453 --> 00:06:13,881
We talk about
an ongoing

216
00:06:13,921 --> 00:06:15,214
process
as a whole,

217
00:06:15,254 --> 00:06:16,977
not as separate
actions.

218
00:06:17,603 --> 00:06:19,414
Think about
what work means.

219
00:06:19,454 --> 00:06:21,411
Work is many
small steps:

220
00:06:21,451 --> 00:06:22,648
answering emails,

221
00:06:22,688 --> 00:06:23,661
making calls,

222
00:06:23,701 --> 00:06:24,924
going to meetings,

223
00:06:24,964 --> 00:06:26,322
writing reports.

224
00:06:26,362 --> 00:06:27,891
We don’t count
each step.

225
00:06:27,931 --> 00:06:29,402
We put one
circle around all

226
00:06:29,442 --> 00:06:31,055
of them,
and call the whole

227
00:06:31,095 --> 00:06:32,713
activity: work.

228
00:06:33,637 --> 00:06:35,341
Research
is the same.

229
00:06:35,381 --> 00:06:37,312
Reading,
asking questions,

230
00:06:37,352 --> 00:06:39,027
collecting data,
testing,

231
00:06:39,067 --> 00:06:40,885
and writing up
the results.

232
00:06:40,925 --> 00:06:42,613
Many steps,
one circle,

233
00:06:42,653 --> 00:06:43,639
one process:

234
00:06:43,679 --> 00:06:44,715
research.

235
00:06:45,637 --> 00:06:46,668
One process,

236
00:06:46,708 --> 00:06:48,080
not separate
actions,

237
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:49,716
so a singular
verb.

238
00:06:49,756 --> 00:06:51,723
‘Her research
is impressive.’

239
00:06:52,870 --> 00:06:54,819
The old school
rule says work,

240
00:06:54,859 --> 00:06:56,429
research,
progress,

241
00:06:56,469 --> 00:06:58,334
travel and exercise
are always

242
00:06:58,374 --> 00:07:00,532
singular.
Skip that.

243
00:07:00,572 --> 00:07:01,573
The logic:

244
00:07:01,613 --> 00:07:03,058
you’re talking
about the whole

245
00:07:03,098 --> 00:07:05,388
activity,
not separate actions.

246
00:07:06,137 --> 00:07:08,368
The same goes for
the whole family.

247
00:07:08,408 --> 00:07:08,924
Work:

248
00:07:08,964 --> 00:07:10,852
the whole activity
of working.

249
00:07:10,892 --> 00:07:11,704
Research:

250
00:07:11,744 --> 00:07:13,525
the whole process
of studying.

251
00:07:13,565 --> 00:07:14,299
Progress:

252
00:07:14,339 --> 00:07:15,829
the process
of improving.

253
00:07:15,869 --> 00:07:16,406
Travel:

254
00:07:16,446 --> 00:07:18,007
going from
place to place.

255
00:07:18,047 --> 00:07:18,695
Exercise:

256
00:07:18,735 --> 00:07:20,471
physical training
in general.

257
00:07:20,511 --> 00:07:22,471
Training:
developing a skill.

258
00:07:22,511 --> 00:07:24,523
Sleep:
the state of sleeping.

259
00:07:24,563 --> 00:07:26,398
Shopping:
buying things.

260
00:07:26,438 --> 00:07:27,356
And business:

261
00:07:27,396 --> 00:07:29,407
buying,
selling and trading.

262
00:07:30,337 --> 00:07:31,652
Now think
about it.

263
00:07:31,692 --> 00:07:33,115
Do we say
‘I do a work’,

264
00:07:33,155 --> 00:07:34,599
or ‘I do work’?

265
00:07:35,937 --> 00:07:37,463
So here’s
the crux.

266
00:07:37,503 --> 00:07:39,785
How do you know
a noun is uncountable?

267
00:07:39,825 --> 00:07:41,399
Check five things.

268
00:07:42,037 --> 00:07:43,735
Does it work
as one unit?

269
00:07:43,775 --> 00:07:45,017
Pens, pencils,

270
00:07:45,057 --> 00:07:46,358
clips and a ruler:

271
00:07:46,398 --> 00:07:48,843
circle them,
and they’re stationery.

272
00:07:49,470 --> 00:07:50,842
Is it a material?

273
00:07:50,882 --> 00:07:52,796
A house made
of stone.

274
00:07:53,337 --> 00:07:55,092
Is it usually
measured rather

275
00:07:55,132 --> 00:07:56,362
than counted?

276
00:07:56,402 --> 00:07:57,911
A jar of honey.

277
00:07:58,437 --> 00:07:59,553
Is it abstract,

278
00:07:59,593 --> 00:08:01,156
with no
physical form?

279
00:08:01,196 --> 00:08:02,099
Peace.

280
00:08:02,637 --> 00:08:04,262
Or is it
an activity,

281
00:08:04,302 --> 00:08:05,700
taken as a whole?

282
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:07,043
Exercise.

283
00:08:07,670 --> 00:08:09,203
If the answer
is yes,

284
00:08:09,243 --> 00:08:10,530
it’s uncountable:

285
00:08:10,570 --> 00:08:13,012
no s,
and a singular verb.

286
00:08:14,337 --> 00:08:15,732
Let’s put all
the examples

287
00:08:15,772 --> 00:08:17,375
on the board.

288
00:08:18,003 --> 00:08:19,329
Unit or mass:

289
00:08:19,369 --> 00:08:20,784
furniture,
luggage,

290
00:08:20,824 --> 00:08:22,239
baggage,
equipment,

291
00:08:22,279 --> 00:08:23,694
machinery,
garbage,

292
00:08:23,734 --> 00:08:24,804
rubbish, clothing,

293
00:08:24,844 --> 00:08:26,044
stationery,
crockery,

294
00:08:26,084 --> 00:08:27,881
cutlery,
scenery and traffic.

295
00:08:27,921 --> 00:08:29,897
Many things,
one unit.

296
00:08:30,637 --> 00:08:32,931
Material: gold,
silver, iron,

297
00:08:32,971 --> 00:08:34,511
copper,
steel, wood,

298
00:08:34,551 --> 00:08:35,989
stone, sand, clay,

299
00:08:36,029 --> 00:08:37,518
cotton,
wool, silk,

300
00:08:37,558 --> 00:08:38,555
leather, plastic,

301
00:08:38,595 --> 00:08:39,528
glass and paper.

302
00:08:39,568 --> 00:08:41,283
It’s what things
are made of.

303
00:08:42,203 --> 00:08:44,032
Measured: food,
rice, bread,

304
00:08:44,072 --> 00:08:45,383
butter,
cheese, meat,

305
00:08:45,423 --> 00:08:46,650
sugar,
flour, salt,

306
00:08:46,690 --> 00:08:47,835
milk, water, tea,

307
00:08:47,875 --> 00:08:48,977
coffee and honey.

308
00:08:49,017 --> 00:08:50,545
Measured
by amount.

309
00:08:51,270 --> 00:08:54,070
Abstract: advice,
information,

310
00:08:54,110 --> 00:08:55,331
knowledge,
education,

311
00:08:55,371 --> 00:08:56,419
honesty, justice,

312
00:08:56,459 --> 00:08:57,614
patience, courage,

313
00:08:57,654 --> 00:08:58,881
wisdom, happiness,

314
00:08:58,921 --> 00:09:00,927
freedom,
peace and evidence.

315
00:09:00,967 --> 00:09:02,492
No physical form.

316
00:09:03,237 --> 00:09:04,981
Activity
or process:

317
00:09:05,021 --> 00:09:06,770
work, research,
progress,

318
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:07,997
travel, exercise,

319
00:09:08,037 --> 00:09:09,173
training, sleep,

320
00:09:09,213 --> 00:09:10,768
shopping
and business.

321
00:09:10,808 --> 00:09:12,515
One whole
activity.

322
00:09:13,437 --> 00:09:15,423
So don’t memorise
the list.

323
00:09:15,463 --> 00:09:16,590
Find the category,

324
00:09:16,630 --> 00:09:18,075
and the logic
tells you:

325
00:09:18,115 --> 00:09:20,528
no s,
and a singular verb.

326
00:09:20,568 --> 00:09:22,811
That’s the Grammar
Logic Series.

